(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cms-exam-1-master-exam-updated-form-e"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 1 Master Exam (Updated) — Form E","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 47-year-old man with alcohol use disorder develops severe leg pain and rapidly progressive skin changes after a minor injury. Which set of risk factors is associated with necrotizing fasciitis?","choices":["Lymphatic impairment, athlete's foot, obesity","Wet work and chemical exposure","Obesity, poor hygiene, occlusive clothing, humidity","Manicure, ingrown nail, and nail biting","Trauma, burns, surgery, immunosuppression, alcoholism"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Those are the listed risk factors, and the condition is more common in males than females. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has multiple light to dark brown macules with well-defined but irregular borders over the shoulders and dorsal hands, tending to coalesce where he has had severe sunburns. They do not fade in winter. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Lentigo maligna","Melasma","Ephelides","Seborrheic keratosis","Solar lentigo"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Solar lentigines arise from proliferation of basal melanocytes with increased melanin production, strongly associated with older age, sun damage, and tanning. They are well-defined with irregular borders and tend to coalesce at sites of severe sunburn. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with toxic epidermal necrolysis is admitted to the burn unit. What scoring system should be calculated within 24 hours and repeated on day three?","choices":["The Fitzpatrick scale","The ankle-brachial index","SCORTEN","The minimal erythema dose","The Parkland formula"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. SCORTEN should be calculated within 24 hours of admission and repeated on day 3. Each variable scores one point, including age over 40 years, presence of malignancy, heart rate over 120 beats per minute, and the initial extent of epidermal detachment. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man with alcohol use disorder has severe leg pain after a minor injury. Over hours the skin has changed from red-purple to blue-grey, bullae with thick purple fluid have appeared, and the area that was exquisitely tender is now numb. He is febrile and tachycardic. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Deep vein thrombosis","Pyoderma gangrenosum","Necrotizing fasciitis","Cellulitis","Erysipelas"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Necrotizing fasciitis progresses rapidly with skin colour changing from red-purple to blue-grey, bullae containing thick pink or purple fluid, and loss of tenderness as superficial nerves are destroyed — an ominous sign. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 127"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with a newly diagnosed melanoma asks whether his lymph nodes will be sampled. At what thresholds is sentinel lymph node biopsy offered or discussed in melanoma?","choices":["Only with palpable nodes","At 1.0 mm, or 0.8 mm with risk","Any thickness","By lesion diameter","Only at 4 mm or greater"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sentinel lymph node biopsy is offered or discussed at 1.0 mm Breslow thickness or greater, or at 0.8 mm or greater when additional histologic risk factors such as ulceration or a high mitotic rate are present. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A clinician estimates the likelihood of a condition in a 60-year-old woman before ordering a test, then revises that estimate once the result returns. How do pretest and posttest probability differ?","choices":["Pretest applies only to screening","Posttest ignores the clinical picture","Pretest is before the result, posttest after","Pretest is after the result, posttest before","They are the same quantity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pretest probability is the likelihood of a condition before the test result is known, informed by signs and symptoms, history and risk factors, and how common the condition is in the population. Posttest probability is the likelihood after the result is known. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man with alcohol use disorder presents with severe leg pain following a minor injury. Over hours the skin changes from red-purple to blue-grey, bullae with thick purple fluid appear, and the area that was exquisitely tender becomes numb. He is febrile and tachycardic. What is the most appropriate immediate action?","choices":["Immediate surgical debridement","Magnetic resonance imaging first","Bedside incision and drainage","Admit for observation and elevate","Antibiotics, reassess in 24 hours"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Necrotizing fasciitis is a surgical emergency with high mortality, and laboratory tests and imaging studies should not delay surgical intervention. Loss of tenderness reflects destruction of superficial nerves and is an ominous sign. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 128"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman presents with burning and small erythematous papules around her mouth. Examination demonstrates grouped monomorphic papules and papulopustules involving the nasolabial folds and chin, with a narrow zone of sparing immediately adjacent to the vermilion border. There are no open or closed comedones. She has applied hydrocortisone cream to her face daily for several months. What is the most appropriate initial management?","choices":["Increase the steroid potency","Stop the steroid; warn of a flare","Add adapalene, continue the steroid","Oral isotretinoin","Stop the steroid; expect no flare"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Topical corticosteroid exposure is the most important modifiable association, so facial steroids should be discontinued because continued exposure perpetuates the condition. Patients must be told the eruption may temporarily worsen after withdrawal, or they will restart the steroid when it does. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man is diagnosed with pityriasis rosea. The eruption began four days ago and is mildly itchy. He is otherwise well and not pregnant-exposed or immunocompromised. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Reassurance alone, no treatment for itch","Oral prednisone over two weeks","Oral acyclovir for all patients","Oral terbinafine","Reassurance, with an antihistamine for itch"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Pityriasis rosea is self-limited and the mainstay is reassurance. Oral antihistamines or topical steroids can be used cautiously for pruritus, and phototherapy or natural sunlight may help if begun in the first week of the eruption. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 171"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man has a purulent collection in the axilla at the site of an old puncture wound, and a student asks how this differs from a furuncle. How does a cutaneous abscess differ in origin from a furuncle?","choices":["Abscess involves apocrine, furuncle eccrine glands","Abscess is sterile; furuncle infected","Abscess from inoculation; furuncle from a follicle","Abscess from a follicle; furuncle from inoculation","Abscess is superficial; furuncle epidermal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An abscess is a collection of purulent material within the dermis and deeper tissues, often due to traumatic inoculation of bacteria into the skin, compared with furuncles, which arise from infected hair follicles. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man still has burning, stabbing pain four months after a thoracic zoster eruption cleared. How is postherpetic neuralgia defined, and what are the first-line treatments?","choices":["Pain beyond 90 days; gabapentin or a tricyclic","Any acute pain; acetaminophen","Pain beyond 90 days; long-term opioids","Pain beyond 7 days; antivirals","Pain beyond 30 days; corticosteroids"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pain persisting 90 days or more after rash onset is the commonly used definition. First-line treatment is gabapentin or pregabalin, an appropriate tricyclic antidepressant, or topical lidocaine, with a capsaicin patch as a further option. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man presents with an abrupt eruption of small pustules on the thighs and buttocks. Each pustule sits on an erythematous base and is pierced by a central hair. He is afebrile with no systemic symptoms. He has type 2 diabetes and wears occlusive workout clothing. What is the most likely causative organism?","choices":["Corynebacterium minutissimum","Streptococcus pyogenes","Staphylococcus aureus","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Candida albicans"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bacterial folliculitis is most commonly caused by Staphylococcus aureus. His diabetes and occlusive clothing are both listed risk factors, alongside obesity, poor hygiene, heat and humidity, immunocompromise, corticosteroids, and nasal carriage. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man presents with an itchy, scaling annular plaque on the trunk. The clinician wishes to confirm the presence of fungal elements at the bedside before starting treatment. What is the most appropriate test?","choices":["Tzanck smear","Patch testing","Mineral oil preparation","Potassium hydroxide wet preparation","Nail clipping with periodic acid-Schiff stain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A potassium hydroxide wet preparation detects fungal elements in skin, hair, or nail samples, and it is the appropriate bedside confirmation before treating a suspected dermatophyte infection. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman has a sharply bounded eruption on the forearm whose outline does not correspond to any anatomical distribution. What general feature most often allows contact dermatitis to be distinguished from other dermatologic conditions on inspection alone?","choices":["Its tendency to produce lesions with central clearing","Its association with a preceding viral prodrome","Its predilection for sebum-rich areas of the body","Its clearly demarcated, unnatural patterns","Its symmetric involvement of flexural surfaces"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Contact dermatitis in general is often distinguishable from other dermatologic conditions because of its clearly demarcated, unnatural patterns — the eruption traces the shape of whatever touched the skin rather than following a biological distribution. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman with deeply pigmented skin asks whether she needs sun protection at all. What does the Fitzpatrick skin type classification predict, and how should it guide counselling?","choices":["The severity of an existing sunburn","The likelihood of rosacea","Baseline sensitivity; types V and VI need none","Baseline ultraviolet sensitivity; all types need care","The required sun protection factor alone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fitzpatrick skin type predicts baseline ultraviolet sensitivity and guides photoprotection counselling. All types are susceptible to cumulative ultraviolet damage, photoaging, and skin cancer, though absolute risk varies. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"A 23-year-old woman has plantar warts that are not painful and do not interfere with walking. What is the appropriate management?","choices":["Surgical excision to remove the roots","Cryotherapy every 2 to 3 weeks regardless of symptoms","Daily salicylic acid 40 percent indefinitely to prevent recurrence","No therapy is required unless they are painful","Oral antiviral therapy to eradicate human papillomavirus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Plantar warts do not require therapy unless they are painful. They can cluster together to form a mosaic wart, and treatment when needed is salicylic acid 40 percent or cryotherapy. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 145"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman has symmetrical, well-defined, completely white non-scaly macules and patches on the dorsal hands and around the mouth. They fluoresce under a Wood's lamp. Her mother has autoimmune thyroid disease. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pityriasis alba","Leprosy","Vitiligo","Pityriasis versicolor","Postinflammatory hypopigmentation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Vitiligo is an autoimmune disease causing depigmentation through T cell mediated destruction of melanocytes, producing asymptomatic white non-scaly macules and patches with distinct margins that fluoresce under a Wood's lamp, usually symmetrical. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman has itchy, discoloured skin over both lower legs. Examination shows poorly demarcated erythematous and brown-pigmented patches in the gaiter region of both legs, with pitting oedema and varicosities. She is afebrile and both legs are affected equally. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Stasis dermatitis","Cellulitis","Contact dermatitis","Erysipelas","Nummular eczema"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Stasis dermatitis follows chronic venous hypertension and produces pruritic erythematous, violaceous, or hyperpigmented patches in the gaiter region of one or both legs, with oedema and brown haemosiderin staining. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 114"},{"q":"A 12-year-old girl has small light brown macules on the nose that fade each winter, while her 68-year-old grandmother has darker macules on the hands and trunk that do not change with the seasons. How does a simple lentigo differ from an ephelis in its relationship to sun exposure?","choices":["Lentigines are always over 2 cm","Lentigines always require excision","Lentigines do not fade seasonally; ephelides do","Lentigines fade in winter; ephelides do not","Lentigines never involve mucosa"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lentigines are benign, well-circumscribed, round to oval uniformly black or brown macules under 5 mm occurring on skin, conjunctiva, and mucocutaneous surfaces, on both sun-exposed and sun-protected areas. Ephelides are most pronounced in spring and summer and fade in winter. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 42-year-old man has recurrent poorly demarcated erythematous patches with greasy yellow scale over the scalp, eyebrows, nasolabial folds, and behind both ears. The itching is worse when he sweats. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Perioral dermatitis","Tinea capitis","Atopic dermatitis","Psoriasis","Seborrheic dermatitis"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Seborrheic dermatitis produces poorly demarcated pruritic erythematous plaques with greasy or yellow scaling at sebum-rich sites, and pruritus is increased by perspiration. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man in Connecticut develops an expanding erythematous oval lesion 8 cm across with central clearing and a darker punctate centre at the site of a recent tick bite. What stage is this, what is the lesion called, and what should be done about testing?","choices":["Stage 2; intravenous ceftriaxone","Stage 1 erythema migrans; treat clinically","Stage 1; wait for two-tier serology","Cellulitis; cephalexin","Stage 3; look for arthritis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stage 1 early localised infection is characterised by erythema migrans, a lesion greater than 5 cm that expands with central clearing and often a darker punctate centre, also called a bull's-eye. A patient with this lesion should be diagnosed and treated on that basis, and testing is most helpful in patients from non-endemic regions with non-diagnostic symptoms. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 3-week-old infant, born prematurely, has a bright red papule on the scalp that was a faint blanched area with fine telangiectasias at birth and has grown noticeably in the past fortnight. What growth pattern should the parents be told to expect?","choices":["Rapid growth, then slow involution","Involution from birth, gone by 6 weeks","No change during childhood","Steady growth with no involution","Rapid growth with malignant risk"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Infantile hemangiomas have a unique growth pattern of rapid proliferation followed by slower involution, with rapid growth during the neonatal period and most growth in the first 4 to 6 months. The earliest sign is blanching of the involved skin followed by fine telangiectasias and then a red macule. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has fine red lines on both cheeks that disappear when pressed with a glass slide. How is a telangiectasia defined, and what is its behaviour on pressure?","choices":["A dilated capillary under 1 mm that blanches","A 1 to 2 mm blood deposit that does not blanch","A congenital malformation","A dilated capillary under 1 mm that does not blanch","A capillary proliferation forming a papule"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Telangiectasias are acquired vascular lesions consisting of permanently dilated capillaries under 1 mm. They blanch, may be single or grouped with a central punctum, may be primary or secondary, and are associated with numerous diseases. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman describes three days of burning pain in a band across her left flank, followed by an eruption. Examination shows grouped vesicles on an erythematous base confined to a single band that stops abruptly at the midline. What are the three clinical phases of this condition?","choices":["Prodromal fever, generalised eruption, desquamation","Pre-eruptive, acute eruptive, postherpetic neuralgia","Erythematous, bullous, and necrotic","Pre-eruptive, eruptive, and scarring","Incubation, contagious, and latency"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The pre-eruptive phase is characterised by dysesthesia or pain within the affected dermatome with lesion onset by 48 to 72 hours; the acute eruptive phase produces grouped herpetiform vesicles on an erythematous base with new lesions forming over 3 to 5 days; and postherpetic neuralgia is the chronic phase. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"A 4-year-old boy has a flat pink-purple patch on one side of the face that has been present since birth and has grown in proportion with him. Which statement best describes a nevus flammeus?","choices":["Appears at six months; proliferates then involutes","Present at birth; darkens and never involutes","A soft yellow lipid plaque","An acquired adult capillary proliferation","Present at birth; involutes by school age"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nevus flammeus is a congenital vascular lesion of dilated superficial dermal capillaries through the entire depth of the dermis, without endothelial proliferation. It is present at birth, grows in size with the child, does not involute, and becomes darker and thicker. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman with hidradenitis suppurativa who smokes and is obese asks what she can do herself to reduce flares. Which measure is described as essential?","choices":["Wearing tight-fitting garments to support the affected areas","Smoking cessation","Daily vigorous scrubbing of the affected areas","Applying heat to the axillae daily","Shaving the affected areas daily with a multi-blade razor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Smoking cessation is described as essential among the preventive measures, alongside avoiding heat exposure, daily cleansing with an antibacterial soap or chlorhexidine or benzoyl peroxide wash, weight loss if obese, and avoiding constrictive clothing and frictional trauma. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 29-year-old dental hygienist develops a painful, swollen index finger with grouped vesicles on an erythematous base, preceded by burning and tingling. A colleague suggests incision and drainage. What is the correct management?","choices":["A topical antifungal and a dry hand","Do not incise; cover it and give antivirals","Observe without treatment or precautions","Incise and drain, then give antivirals","Incise and drain, then give antibiotics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Incision and drainage does not treat herpes simplex virus and can delay healing. Lesions should be covered, hand hygiene used, contact with mucosa and broken skin avoided until healed, and early oral acyclovir, valacyclovir, or famciclovir may shorten the episode. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 128"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man has confirmed tungiasis of both feet after a trip abroad. What management is appropriate?","choices":["Excision or cryotherapy, with tetanus cover","Whole-body overnight permethrin","Antihistamines and oatmeal baths alone","Oral albendazole for 3 days","Professional extermination of the home"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Management includes surgical excision or cryotherapy and topical agents, tetanus prophylaxis, and systemic antibiotics. Prevention centres on avoiding walking barefoot in endemic areas. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 21-year-old woman presents with painful genital ulcers. The clinician considers alternative causes. Which differential is characteristically painless?","choices":["Herpes simplex recurrence","Syphilis","Chancroid","Trauma","Candidiasis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Syphilis lesions start as solitary raised papules that erode and are usually painless, which is what distinguishes them from the painful ulcers of herpes simplex virus and chancroid. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 121"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man has several soft whitish-yellow papules 3 to 5 mm on the forehead, each with a central dell. He is worried they are skin cancer. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Molluscum contagiosum","Syringoma","Basal cell carcinoma","Sebaceous hyperplasia","Xanthelasma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sebaceous hyperplasia is a common benign condition of enlarged sebaceous glands from changing sebocyte turnover with age, presenting as single or multiple whitish-yellow or skin-coloured soft papules 2 to 9 mm with a central dell. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"A 32-year-old fair-skinned woman has numerous moles under 6 mm with homogeneous surface and colour. One lesion is very dark brown to black. What is the significance of that lesion?","choices":["It is expected with many moles; no action","It is suspicious and warrants evaluation","It is a Spitz nevus; always benign","It confirms melanoma without biopsy","It is a blue nevus; no evaluation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Common acquired melanocytic naevi are usually less than 6 mm with homogeneous surface and colour, round to oval with sharp demarcation. A very dark brown or black lesion on a light-skinned individual is specifically flagged as suspicious. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman has a sacral wound showing full-thickness loss of skin extending into the dermis, and a second area on the heel showing partial loss involving only the epidermis. What are these two findings called?","choices":["Excoriation and lichenification, respectively","Ulceration and erosion, respectively","Erosion and ulceration, respectively","Scar and scale, respectively","Fissure and excoriation, respectively"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ulceration is full-thickness loss of the skin extending to the dermis or deeper, whereas erosion is partial loss of the skin of only the epidermis. Depth is the entire distinction. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man has a moveable, round, firm nodule on the back with a visible central pore. It occasionally discharges foul-smelling material. It is not currently red or tender. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pilar cyst","Lipoma","Abscess","Dermatofibroma","Epidermoid cyst"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. An epidermoid cyst is a cystic enclosure of epithelium within the dermis filled with keratin, presenting as a single moveable round protruding mass with a central pore or punctum communicating with the surface. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 5-year-old boy is diagnosed with varicella and his parents ask when he may return to school. For how long is a child with varicella considered contagious?","choices":["From 1 to 2 days before the rash until all lesions have crusted","For 21 days from exposure regardless of the rash","From the appearance of the rash until all lesions have crusted","Until fever resolves, regardless of the state of the lesions","Only while vesicles are present, ending once any crusting begins"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patients are contagious from 1 to 2 days before the rash appears until all lesions crust. In breakthrough disease without crusts, the period runs until no new lesions have appeared for 24 hours. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 16-year-old boy has lesions on the face, upper back, and chest for a year. Examination shows open and closed comedones together with inflammatory papules and pustules, and a few nodules. There is no flushing and no telangiectasia. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Perioral dermatitis","Acne rosacea","Folliculitis","Acne vulgaris","Pseudofolliculitis barbae"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acne vulgaris is polymorphic, with comedones as the hallmark lesion alongside papules, pustules, and nodules, affecting areas with hormonally responsive sebaceous glands — the face, neck, upper arms, back, and chest. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man has confirmed toxic epidermal necrolysis with epidermal detachment of 45 percent of his body surface area. What is the mandatory disposition?","choices":["A burn unit or intensive care unit","A surgical ward for debridement","Outpatient management","Emergency department observation","A general medical ward"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Burn unit or intensive care admission is mandatory for all cases of toxic epidermal necrolysis, together with immediate discontinuation of all suspect drugs, fluid resuscitation estimated as for major burns, and non-adhesive biological dressings. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man in Connecticut has an expanding erythematous oval lesion 9 cm across with central clearing and a darker punctate centre at the site of a recent tick bite. He has mild fatigue. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Lyme disease","Granuloma annulare","Rocky Mountain spotted fever","Tinea corporis","Cellulitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Stage 1 early localised Lyme disease is characterised by erythema migrans: a large expanding erythematous round or oval lesion over 5 cm with central clearing and often a darker punctate centre at the bite site, also called a bull's-eye. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has newly diagnosed mycosis fungoides confined to patches and plaques on the trunk, and his family asks why he has not been offered chemotherapy. What is the treatment philosophy in mycosis fungoides?","choices":["Stage-directed, skin-directed therapy","No treatment at any stage","Immediate combination chemotherapy","Indefinite high-dose corticosteroids","Excision of all involved skin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Management is complex. Early aggressive treatment has not been proven to cure disease or prevent progression, and overly aggressive therapy may cause complications and premature death, so treatment is stage-directed and skin-directed. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has an itchy papular eruption two days after swimming in a freshwater lake. What is the management of cercarial dermatitis?","choices":["Antihistamines and oatmeal baths","Extermination of the water source","Whole-body overnight permethrin","Oral albendazole for 3 days","Doxycycline for 5 to 10 days"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Management is symptomatic: antihistamines, oatmeal baths, antipruritic lotions, aspirin for pain control, proper washing and hygiene, and topical or oral glucocorticoids. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man reports six weeks of intense itching that is worst at night. Examination shows excoriated papules with thin thread-like linear burrows in the finger webs, volar wrists, and axillae. His partner has begun to itch as well. The head and neck are spared. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Scabies","Dermatitis herpetiformis","Pediculosis corporis","Bedbug bites","Atopic dermatitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Scabies produces almost always severe itching with intense nocturnal pruritus appearing 4 to 6 weeks after infestation, favouring the interdigital webs, volar wrists, axillae, and genitals with the head and neck spared. The thread-like burrow is pathognomonic. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman has several beige to dark brown papules and plaques 5 to 15 mm on the trunk that feel velvety and appear pasted onto the skin surface. They have appeared gradually over years. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Actinic keratosis","Malignant melanoma","Melanocytic naevus","Solar lentigo","Seborrheic keratosis"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Seborrheic keratoses are benign papules and plaques, beige to brown to black, 2 to 20 mm, feeling velvety or warty and appearing stuck or pasted onto the skin. They are common in older adults and easily mistaken for neoplasms. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man presents with a new benign lesion on a sun-exposed area of the forearm. What opportunity does this create?","choices":["Counselling on sunscreen and self-examination","Prophylactic excision of all lesions","Reassurance alone","Referral for full-body photography","Advising complete avoidance of outdoors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. When discussing any new lesion in sun-exposed areas, the clinician should take the opportunity to counsel on sunscreen use, avoiding direct sun during peak hours, and performing periodic skin self-examinations. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A student uses the VINDICATE mnemonic to build a differential for a 72-year-old man brought to the emergency department with confusion. What do the letters stand for?","choices":["A checklist for appraising evidence","A physical examination sequence","A pretest probability score","Vascular, inflammatory, neurological, drug-related, idiopathic, cardiac, allergic, toxic, environmental","Vascular, infectious, neoplastic, degenerative, iatrogenic, congenital, autoimmune, trauma, endocrine"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. VINDICATE is a category-based framework: vascular, infectious, neoplastic, degenerative, iatrogenic, congenital, autoimmune, trauma, and endocrine or metabolic. Applied to confusion it yields stroke, meningitis, brain tumour, Alzheimer disease, and so on. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman with dermatitis herpetiformis achieves rapid relief on dapsone and asks whether that is the whole treatment. What is the most important additional element of management?","choices":["A gluten-free diet for six weeks","Indefinite dapsone monotherapy","High-potency topical steroids alone","Lifelong systemic corticosteroids","A strict lifelong gluten-free diet"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Dapsone provides rapid symptom relief but the long-term management is a strict gluten-free diet, because the disease is driven by immunoglobulin A antibodies against epidermal transglutaminase in patients consuming dietary gluten. All patients should also be screened for celiac disease. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with diabetes has an asymptomatic brownish patch in the crural region and scaling between the fourth and fifth toes. Under a Wood's lamp the affected skin fluoresces coral-red. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Contact dermatitis","Tinea cruris","Erythrasma","Inverse psoriasis","Cutaneous candidiasis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Erythrasma is a chronic superficial infection of intertriginous skin by Corynebacterium minutissimum, favouring the inner thighs, crural region, scrotum, and fourth toe web, and coral-red fluorescence under a Wood's lamp is diagnostic. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man treated for scabies returns with new pustules and continuing pruritus. What complications of scabies should be anticipated?","choices":["Systemic parasitic infection of internal organs","Superinfection and post-scabietic papules","Permanent scarring alopecia","Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis in all","Malignant transformation of the burrow"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Staphylococcal superinfection may lead to sepsis and requires antibiotics. Persistent pruritic post-scabietic papules are treated with mid to high potency corticosteroids or intralesional triamcinolone acetonide. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 21-year-old woman returns from a tropical beach holiday with an intensely itchy, raised, serpentine track on the foot that has advanced a few centimetres over the past two days. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tungiasis","Cutaneous larva migrans","Scabies","Cercarial dermatitis","Contact dermatitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cutaneous larva migrans results from animal hookworm larvae in which the human is a dead-end host, producing an erythematous, raised, vesicular, linear or serpentine cutaneous trail that progresses 2 to 3 cm per day with intense pruritus. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman presents three days after a manicure with rapid onset of redness, swelling, and tenderness of the tissue around her right index fingernail. Purulent fluid has collected beneath the skin of the nail fold. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Herpetic whitlow","Acute paronychia","Onychomycosis","Felon","Chronic paronychia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute paronychia is infection of the soft tissue around the nail, usually 2 to 5 days after trauma such as a manicure, ingrown nail, hangnail, or nail biting. It starts as cellulitis and progresses to abscess. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 117"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man develops severe nocturnal itching six weeks after his partner was treated for scabies. How is scabies typically transmitted, and how long after infestation does pruritus appear?","choices":["Head-to-head contact; itch at 4 to 6 weeks","Brief casual contact; itch within 24 hours","Contaminated water; itch within hours","Close contact 15 to 20 minutes; itch at 4 weeks","Airborne spread; itch after 1 week"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Scabies is transmitted by close physical contact for 15 to 20 minutes and may also be acquired from the bedding or underclothing of an infested individual. Pruritus appears 4 to 6 weeks after initial infestation, and many patients do not develop symptoms for up to 3 months. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman with biopsy-confirmed erythema nodosum has bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy on chest radiograph together with fever and arthralgia. What is the most appropriate referral?","choices":["Haematology, for probable lymphoma","Infectious disease, for probable endemic mycosis","Dermatology, for repeat biopsy","Pulmonology or rheumatology, for probable Löfgren syndrome","Gastroenterology, for probable inflammatory bowel disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Löfgren syndrome is the association referred to pulmonology or rheumatology. The combination of erythema nodosum, bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, fever, and arthralgia is its recognised presentation. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has a small area of cellulitis of the forearm with minimal pain, no systemic signs, and no comorbidity. In which patient with cellulitis is no diagnostic workup required?","choices":["Any cellulitis of the lower leg","Only patients under 40","Limited area, no systemic signs","Any patient already on antibiotics","Any patient who is afebrile"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cellulitis is usually a clinical diagnosis, and no workup is needed when there is a limited area of involvement, minimal pain, no systemic signs, and no risk factor for serious illness such as extremes of age or immunocompromise. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man is diagnosed with dermatitis herpetiformis and dapsone is planned for rapid symptom control. What must be checked before the drug is started, and what must be monitored afterwards?","choices":["Check liver function; monitor for photosensitivity","Check thyroid function; monitor blood count","Check glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase; monitor blood count","No testing or monitoring needed","Check glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase; monitor renal function"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency must be checked before initiating dapsone, and the complete blood count is monitored for haemolytic anaemia and methaemoglobinaemia. Dapsone at 25 to 200 mg daily gives relief within days. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man has a pigmented lesion on the upper back that his wife says has changed. Examination shows an 11 mm lesion whose halves do not match, with a notched border and brown, black, and blue-grey areas within it. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Seborrheic keratosis","Solar lentigo","Pigmented basal cell carcinoma","Malignant melanoma","Dysplastic naevus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lesion meets the ABCDE criteria: asymmetry, an irregular notched border, colour variegation with brown, black, and blue within one lesion, a diameter over 6 mm, and evolution reported by a family member. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has folliculitis of the thighs that has failed to respond to antibacterial treatment, and a hair is plucked from an affected follicle. What test should be performed on it?","choices":["A Wood's lamp examination","A potassium hydroxide wet mount","A mineral oil preparation","A Tzanck smear","Direct immunofluorescence"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In resistant cases a potassium hydroxide wet mount using a plucked hair is used to rule out fungal, specifically dermatophyte, folliculitis. Culture and Gram stain from an unroofed pustule are also performed. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man with untreated human immunodeficiency virus infection has multiple red-purple macules and plaques on the trunk. What examination is described as essential, and what is the first management priority?","choices":["Skin examination alone; mucosa is spared","Oral examination; start or optimise antiretroviral therapy","Oral examination; start chemotherapy first","Lymph node examination; observation","Ophthalmological examination; intralesional vincristine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Oral examination is essential when Kaposi sarcoma is suspected, since hard palate lesions are common and may be the presenting site. For acquired immunodeficiency syndrome-associated disease the first priority is to begin or optimise antiretroviral therapy, because immune restoration is the cornerstone of treatment. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man has scaly annular plaques of the groin and scaling between the toes, and a student asks how these infections are named. How are dermatophyte infections classified, and what does the naming convey?","choices":["By depth of dermal invasion","By species: Trichophyton, Microsporum, Epidermophyton","By body location: capitis, corporis, cruris, pedis","By the patient's age","By whether it is contagious"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dermatophyte infections are classified by body location: tinea capitis for scalp, barbae for beard, corporis for body, manuum for hand, cruris for the inguinal creases, and pedis for feet. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 37-year-old woman has a firm nodule on the lower leg and reports that it occasionally hurts when knocked. What is notable about the symptom profile of a dermatofibroma?","choices":["Invariably painful","Always pruritic, never painful","Symptomatic only if over 3 cm","The commonest painful skin tumour","Painful only after malignant change"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Dermatofibroma is usually asymptomatic, sometimes with a history of insect bite, and when symptomatic may cause slight pruritus or pain — it is described as the most common painful skin tumour. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 19-year-old man develops an intensely pruritic vesicular eruption in linear streaks two days after clearing brush. What immunological mechanism underlies allergic contact dermatitis, and what is its most common cause in the United States?","choices":["Immediate type I reaction to urushiol","Delayed type IV reaction, most often to nickel","Direct barrier disruption, no immune role","Delayed type IV reaction, most often to urushiol","A type III immune complex reaction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Allergic contact dermatitis is an immunologic cell-mediated, delayed, type IV hypersensitivity reaction. Urushiol sap of Toxicodendron species, found in poison ivy, poison sumac, and poison oak, is the most common cause, and roughly 50 to 75 percent of people in the United States are allergic to these plants. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man has three smooth, round bald patches on the scalp that appeared over a month. The underlying scalp is smooth without scale, redness, or scarring, and short hairs narrow at the root are visible at the margins. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tinea capitis","Alopecia areata","Trichotillomania","Androgenetic alopecia","Lichen planopilaris"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Alopecia areata produces sudden round 1 to 4 cm patches of non-scarring hair loss with a smooth scalp, and exclamation point hairs at the margins are pathognomonic. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 138"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has a biopsy-proven superficial basal cell carcinoma on the trunk with no high-risk features and would prefer to avoid surgery. For which basal cell carcinoma is topical therapy an appropriate option, and what must follow it?","choices":["High-risk facial tumours","Any tumour the patient wants left alone","Recurrent tumours after excision","All nodular tumours, no follow-up","Superficial disease, with clearance confirmed"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Topical therapy is used for selected superficial basal cell carcinoma — imiquimod five nights weekly for 6 to 10 weeks or fluorouracil twice daily for up to 12 weeks — and clinical clearance must be confirmed after treatment. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man develops an extremely pruritic papulovesicular eruption on the fingers ten days after being diagnosed with tinea pedis. A potassium hydroxide preparation from the finger lesions is negative, while one from the toe webs is positive. What three criteria establish this diagnosis?","choices":["Positive bacterial culture, responds to antibiotics","Dermatophyte elsewhere, none at the site, resolves","Symmetric, painless, self-resolving","Positive Wood's lamp, negative scraping, biopsy","Dermatophyte elsewhere, fungal elements at the site"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Those are the three criteria for establishing an id reaction. The primary site is potassium hydroxide positive and the distant id site is potassium hydroxide negative, and the eruption occurs 1 to 2 weeks after the primary infection. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman develops an itchy eruption of 2 to 5 mm erythematous papules on the décolletage and forearms a few hours after her first sunny weekend of the spring. The face, which is exposed year-round, is unaffected. What additional test is considered mandatory?","choices":["Photopatch testing to identify a photoallergen","An antinuclear antibody panel to exclude lupus","Serum tryptase to exclude mastocytosis","Skin biopsy in every patient before treatment","A potassium hydroxide preparation of the affected skin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An antinuclear antibody panel is mandatory to exclude lupus, particularly anti-Ro and anti-La antibodies. Polymorphous light eruption is otherwise largely a clinical diagnosis based on history and morphology. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man has a suspicious pigmented growth on the back that the clinician wishes to sample in a way that removes the entire lesion with deep and wide margins. What biopsy technique is appropriate?","choices":["Fine needle aspiration of the lesion","Curettage of the lesion surface","Punch biopsy","Shave biopsy","Excisional biopsy"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. An excisional biopsy removes the whole lesion taking all deep and wide margins, and always needs stitches to close the wound. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 23-year-old woman presents with the sudden appearance of two smooth, round bald patches on her scalp, each about 3 cm across, that developed over three weeks. The underlying scalp is smooth without scaling, erythema, or scarring. Short hairs that are narrow at the root and normal in calibre at the tip are visible at the margins. She has a history of eczema and her mother has hypothyroidism. What finding is pathognomonic for the diagnosis?","choices":["Absence of scarring","Exclamation point hairs","A history of atopy","Round patches of loss","Sudden onset over three weeks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Exclamation point hairs are pathognomonic for alopecia areata. Their growth ability is changing in real time, which produces a hair that is thinner at the root but normal in calibre at the top — exactly the description given here. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 138"},{"q":"A 46-year-old runner has a hyperkeratotic lesion on the sole, and a student asks how to tell a callus from a wart at the bedside. How do the skin lines behave in a callus compared with a verruca vulgaris, and why does this matter?","choices":["Lines cannot be assessed on the sole","Lines run through both","Lines are broken by a callus, run through a wart","Lines run through a callus but are broken by a wart","Lines are broken by both"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Skin lines run through both corns and calluses, whereas verruca vulgaris interrupts the skin lines. This is a bedside sign that requires no equipment. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 8"}]};
